1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Hitler had made a fortress of Europe.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The Allies were counting on engineering advances to help reach it.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Rockets, a menagerie of specialized tanks,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
the world's first computer,

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
and other technological innovations paved the way to the biggest invasion in history.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, D-Day Tech on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,360
Spring 1942.

8
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,000
Continental Western Europe was under German control.

9
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:48,060
Only England stood free.

10
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,780
The English Channel was still in effective mode against Nazi aggression.

11
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:55,540
Hitler had turned his attention to the Eastern Front

12
00:00:55,541 --> 00:00:57,700
and was in a monumental battle with the Russians.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:09,720
America, having just entered the war, and Russia, were anxious to open a Western Front.

14
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:11,600
But the British were reluctant.

15
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:20,020
They had taken enormous casualties in World War I, and therefore Churchill had horrors

16
00:01:20,021 --> 00:01:24,380
about full-scale battles on the Western Front all over again.

17
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:29,720
So they advocated what became known as the peripheral policy, which is you nibble away

18
00:01:29,721 --> 00:01:32,020
at the boundaries of the German Empire,

19
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,300
continue strategic bombing,

20
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,500
and that would be the best way to defeat Germany.

21
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:41,040
The British approach prevailed in 1942.

22
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:43,780
A second front would have to wait.

23
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:46,860
It's fortunate because, quite frankly,

24
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:50,540
we were building the army that was going to win the war,

25
00:01:50,541 --> 00:01:52,800
but it was only going to be able to do that in 1944.

26
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:56,120
In 1942, we were a skeletal force.

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,020
We didn't have the manpower.

28
00:01:58,180 --> 00:01:59,200
We didn't have the technology.

29
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:06,460
During the summer of 1942, a one-day raid along a 10-mile stretch of the French coast

30
00:02:06,461 --> 00:02:11,540
that included the town of Dieppe was organized to test Allied readiness to pull off an invasion.

31
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:17,400
It was primarily a British managed operation.

32
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:20,800
It was a Canadian division with some Britishers and some American Rangers,

33
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:25,120
and they attempted to storm the port of Dieppe in August of 1942.

34
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:34,160
And it was repulsive with large-scale Canadian casualties.

35
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:40,820
And large numbers of prisoners of war taken, and the whole thing was simply a disaster.

36
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:49,360
Dieppe raid proved that there was a certain amount of foolishness in attempting to capture

37
00:02:49,361 --> 00:02:53,980
one of the heavily defended, heavily fortified port cities on the English Channel coast.

38
00:02:53,981 --> 00:03:01,980
Therefore, the idea was to land on a shoreline in a largely undefended region.

39
00:03:01,981 --> 00:03:05,980
Obviously, we know now that Normandy was a site that ultimately was chosen.

40
00:03:05,981 --> 00:03:11,980
But when you do that, you have to have equipment which is geared up to that kind of a job,

41
00:03:11,981 --> 00:03:15,980
and that's where in 1942 this kind of equipment was largely missing.

42
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,980
Even though there was no specific plan or timetable in place,

43
00:03:20,981 --> 00:03:24,980
place, the design and manufacture of the tools of invasion began.

44
00:03:24,981 --> 00:03:30,980
A disaster at Dieppe made it clear that to successfully mount a cross-channel invasion,

45
00:03:30,981 --> 00:03:35,980
it would have to be on a scale unprecedented in the history of warfare.

46
00:03:35,981 --> 00:03:41,980
It was up to the industrial might of America to mass-produce the enormous numbers of weapons,

47
00:03:41,981 --> 00:03:46,980
transport ships, landing craft and vehicles needed to land on open beaches in France,

48
00:03:46,981 --> 00:03:49,700
and confront the German army on the fields of Europe.

49
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:55,300
American-produced equipment began to pour into England in staggering amounts.

50
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:00,780
It was often joked that Britain was going to sink under the weight of the growing stockpiles.

51
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:05,540
The British, not having America's capacity for mass production,

52
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:09,480
focused on developing smaller numbers of specialized equipment,

53
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:14,840
including an array of modified tanks created by the 79th Armored Division

54
00:04:14,841 --> 00:04:17,580
under the command of Major General Percy Hobart.

55
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:22,060
These inventive contraptions became known as Hobart's funnies,

56
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:25,640
and many were designed specifically to help the invaders get off the beaches

57
00:04:25,641 --> 00:04:27,600
and past German obstacles.

58
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:34,360
Now, Hobart had designed different tanks to perform different duties,

59
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:38,420
such as the bobbin, which had this huge roll of canvas, if you will.

60
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:43,660
You could get off the landing craft and then unroll this mat,

61
00:04:43,661 --> 00:04:46,700
and across this mat other tanks can follow.

62
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:50,520
You can now navigate off soft ground or impossible ground

63
00:04:50,521 --> 00:04:51,660
that you couldn't otherwise move.

64
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:54,840
Another one were the fascines.

65
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,360
These were tanks which carried logs.

66
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:00,280
These logs were used to fill in tank traps

67
00:05:00,281 --> 00:05:04,120
so that other vehicles could move along behind them.

68
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:10,860
One of the most important of the Hobart funnies

69
00:05:10,861 --> 00:05:12,620
was based on the Sherman tank.

70
00:05:12,621 --> 00:05:16,380
This was called the Crab. It was a flail tank.

71
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:21,660
It had two projecting arms mounted on the side of the tank sticking out front,

72
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:23,740
like the mounts of a bulldozer blade.

73
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:26,440
And on this was a rotating drum.

74
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:31,220
And on this drum were lengths of chains.

75
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:34,960
And these chains would just fly, which is what they called a flail.

76
00:05:34,961 --> 00:05:38,620
They would flail around, and they would strike the ground with such force

77
00:05:38,621 --> 00:05:42,720
that they would explode German anti-tank teller mines

78
00:05:42,721 --> 00:05:44,520
just from the concussion of the hitting,

79
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:46,620
so they'd clear a path in front of the tank.

80
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:54,060
Most of Hobart's machines proved too eccentric for the American army.

81
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:57,740
The only one they decided to use was the duplex drive tank.

82
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:02,900
It was a Sherman tank with standard treads for driving across land

83
00:06:02,901 --> 00:06:08,100
and a set of propellers that could be engaged to move the tank across water.

84
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:18,860
Of course, all of that's great, but the Sherman tank is a 37-ton vehicle, so even with propellers, you have to have a means of floating this thing.

85
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:27,740
So what they developed is a canvas screen on struts that you can raise once the tank is about to go into the water.

86
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:31,960
They had a wire frame, kind of like a 19th century hoop skirt.

87
00:06:31,961 --> 00:06:40,160
once you're in the water you can go at about four knots which is not very fast

88
00:06:40,161 --> 00:06:48,120
and the idea is to just get on land british inventiveness was also directed at finding

89
00:06:48,121 --> 00:06:54,000
a solution for one of the biggest problems of an open beach invasion how to get tons of supplies

90
00:06:54,001 --> 00:07:01,120
and thousands of vehicles off ships and onto dry land the british came up with the idea of creating

91
00:07:01,121 --> 00:07:05,453
artificial harbors off the beaches. This was another benefit of the D-Ep-Raid.

92
00:07:05,454 --> 00:07:06,154
They knew we

93
00:07:06,155 --> 00:07:07,887
wouldn't catch a port real quick.

94
00:07:07,888 --> 00:07:11,100
We'd have to conquer it from the land, and we needed to have

95
00:07:11,101 --> 00:07:15,497
supplies coming in over open beaches for an extended period of time.

96
00:07:15,498 --> 00:07:16,920
In the summer of 1943,

97
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:23,620
construction began on two enormous modular harbors, codenamed Mulberries, that would be

98
00:07:23,621 --> 00:07:27,271
transported in pieces to Normandy once the invasion began.

99
00:07:27,272 --> 00:07:29,160
Their various parts were built

100
00:07:29,161 --> 00:07:30,560
all over England and Scotland.

101
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:33,220
Huge concrete blocks soon appeared

102
00:07:33,221 --> 00:07:34,260
along the Thames River.

103
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:37,480
And German aerial reconnaissance

104
00:07:37,481 --> 00:07:38,760
saw these being built

105
00:07:38,761 --> 00:07:40,520
and they had no idea what they were.

106
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:42,960
They thought they were any aircraft forts.

107
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:44,520
They didn't know what to make of them

108
00:07:44,521 --> 00:07:46,880
and they never thought about an artificial harbor.

109
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:49,480
As work progressed on the Mulberries,

110
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:52,880
the Western Allies continued to amass their equipment

111
00:07:52,881 --> 00:07:54,960
and train their troops for invasion.

112
00:07:55,820 --> 00:07:56,540
But meanwhile,

113
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:01,300
Russia continued to take tremendous casualties in its epic battle on the Eastern Front.

114
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:07,360
In November of 1943, Franklin Roosevelt, Prime Minister Churchill,

115
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:12,300
met with Stalin to Iran to discuss post-war settlements,

116
00:08:13,180 --> 00:08:16,220
to discuss the Far East, for example,

117
00:08:16,221 --> 00:08:20,680
but also what came up was the invasion, the long-promised invasion.

118
00:08:21,300 --> 00:08:25,360
And Stalin repeatedly expressed doubts as to whether the Americans and British

119
00:08:25,361 --> 00:08:28,813
were serious about this. They had put it off so many times.

120
00:08:28,814 --> 00:08:30,920
And finally, it kind of came down to

121
00:08:30,921 --> 00:08:36,460
the matter, Stalin says, if you're serious about this, you will name a commander of this invasion.

122
00:08:36,540 --> 00:08:37,298
Have you yet?

123
00:08:37,299 --> 00:08:42,080
Which prompted Roosevelt then to make his decision to appoint Dwight D. Eisenhower

124
00:08:42,081 --> 00:08:49,040
as the overall commander of the invasion. With Eisenhower's appointment as supreme

125
00:08:49,041 --> 00:08:54,908
commander of the Allied Expeditionary Forces. The planning for D-Day intensified.

126
00:08:54,909 --> 00:08:55,609
Codenamed

127
00:08:55,610 --> 00:09:01,160
Operation Overlord, it had a location, the French coast at Normandy, and a timetable.

128
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:08,640
Late spring of 1944, a major deception campaign was undertaken to feed the Germans misinformation

129
00:09:08,641 --> 00:09:11,400
about the time and location of the invasion.

130
00:09:11,401 --> 00:09:19,240
They start Operation Fortitude, and the goal there is to convince the Germans of a couple

131
00:09:19,241 --> 00:09:20,240
of things.

132
00:09:20,241 --> 00:09:23,520
One, that we're going to land at the Pas de Calais, which is only 20 miles across the

133
00:09:23,521 --> 00:09:29,860
channel, and the other, that we have such a massive force that the initial landing, that's

134
00:09:29,861 --> 00:09:32,060
only just the tip of the spear.

135
00:09:32,061 --> 00:09:33,060
There's more to come.

136
00:09:33,061 --> 00:09:38,800
A pretend army division was created, called the 1st U.S. Army Group.

137
00:09:38,801 --> 00:09:44,380
FUSAG only existed on paper, in fake radio traffic, and some specially built props.

138
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:50,240
We bring in set designers from the London Theater and from Hollywood.

139
00:09:50,241 --> 00:09:55,420
Their job is to build fake trucks and planes.

140
00:09:55,880 --> 00:10:02,760
They have inflatable tanks, which you can inflate, and all of a sudden, in a field, you can have a whole armored division,

141
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:06,620
which you conveniently let a German observation plane fly overhead and see.

142
00:10:06,621 --> 00:10:10,120
and after the plane leaves, you can deflate this division,

143
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:12,960
move it down the road and inflate it somewhere else.

144
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:16,940
A great deal of effort went into the details of the Fortitude Deception.

145
00:10:17,940 --> 00:10:21,880
But it was up to top-secret technology to prove the Germans were taking the bait.

146
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:29,020
The secret code-breaking operations outside of London at Bletchley Park

147
00:10:29,021 --> 00:10:31,820
had been deciphering German radio messages,

148
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:34,920
encrypted by the Enigma machine for most of the war.

149
00:10:36,620 --> 00:10:48,949
Just a little after the outbreak of the war in Europe in 1939, British code breakers, with the assistance of Polish and French cryptanalysts, had managed to

150
00:10:48,950 --> 00:10:51,320
reconstruct an Enigma machine.

151
00:10:51,321 --> 00:11:05,321
Looking very much like an electric typewriter, the internal workings of the Enigma, its wiring, its rotors and wheels, was able to convert plain text into ciphertext

152
00:11:06,399 --> 00:11:09,780
as an operator typed on the keyboard.

153
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:19,100
But the German high command often entrusted their communications to an even more complex encrypting device, the Lorenz machine.

154
00:11:19,101 --> 00:11:26,100
It produced an encoded message in the form of a teletype-style paper tape.

155
00:11:26,101 --> 00:11:32,200
In 1944, in time to confirm that the Germans were falling for the fortitude deceptions,

156
00:11:32,201 --> 00:11:35,700
a new piece of equipment was installed at Bletchley Park.

157
00:11:35,701 --> 00:11:39,220
It filled an entire room with wires and vacuum tubes.

158
00:11:39,221 --> 00:11:46,260
It was the first programmable computer, aptly named Colossus.

159
00:11:46,261 --> 00:11:54,700
What Colossus did was read 5,000 teletype characters per second from tape.

160
00:11:55,600 --> 00:12:01,900
And so it reduced the time needed to solve Lorenz from weeks into hours.

161
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:03,720
This is your first true computer.

162
00:12:03,721 --> 00:12:10,700
In other words, the Second World War, the preparations for D-Day ushered in the computer age.

163
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:15,520
Across the channel, the Germans sensed invasion was imminent.

164
00:12:16,260 --> 00:12:23,260
Field Marshal Erwin Rommel, recently assigned the task of strengthening the coastal defenses, known as the Atlantic Wall,

165
00:12:23,261 --> 00:12:32,260
tried to cover as much of the coastline as possible, with a grotesque entanglement of metal objects designed to block the landing craft of an amphibious assault.

166
00:12:34,260 --> 00:12:37,260
Mines were attached to many of the objects in the water.

167
00:12:37,261 --> 00:12:44,260
Above the beaches were gun emplacements, ranging from simple machine gun nests to powerful artillery.

168
00:12:44,261 --> 00:12:51,260
Behind the beaches, some fields were flooded to entrap paratroopers.

169
00:12:51,261 --> 00:12:58,260
In others, wooden poles were erected to defend against glider landings.

170
00:12:58,261 --> 00:13:05,260
Allied reconnaissance of the Atlantic wall defenses made the men of the invasion force aware of what they were about to face

171
00:13:05,261 --> 00:13:13,260
as they gathered at the airfields and loaded onto the flotilla that would take them to Normandy.

172
00:13:13,261 --> 00:13:26,260
D-Day was a tremendous gamble, but technologies were in place to help, including everything from ragdoll decoys to engineless aircraft and complex electronic trickery.

173
00:13:28,260 --> 00:13:39,260
By June 6, 1944, over 4 million mines have been installed along the Atlantic Wall coastal defenses, most under Field Marshal Rommel's direction.

174
00:13:39,261 --> 00:13:42,260
D-Day Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

175
00:13:43,260 --> 00:13:48,260
We now return to D-Day Tech on Modern Marvels.

176
00:13:48,261 --> 00:13:55,260
On the night of June 5th and in the early hours of June 6th,

177
00:13:55,261 --> 00:13:58,260
as the mammoth armada began to make its way to Normandy

178
00:13:58,261 --> 00:14:01,260
for the biggest amphibious assault in history,

179
00:14:01,261 --> 00:14:04,260
the deception campaigns continued.

180
00:14:04,261 --> 00:14:08,260
In preparation for D-Day, strategic bombing raids

181
00:14:08,261 --> 00:14:11,260
had rained devastation on German defenses

182
00:14:11,261 --> 00:14:13,340
and transportation infrastructure.

183
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:16,780
But special planning went into targeting radar installations.

184
00:14:19,320 --> 00:14:21,480
Now, some of those radar installations were bombed.

185
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:22,720
Some of them were not bombed

186
00:14:22,721 --> 00:14:24,400
because they were going to be used

187
00:14:24,401 --> 00:14:26,660
as a part of the overall deception plan

188
00:14:26,661 --> 00:14:27,740
that would convince the Germans

189
00:14:27,741 --> 00:14:30,040
that the operation was not going to come ashore in Normandy,

190
00:14:30,360 --> 00:14:31,180
but somewhere else.

191
00:14:32,220 --> 00:14:33,740
In a brilliantly devised ploy,

192
00:14:34,440 --> 00:14:36,880
German radar technology was tricked into indicating

193
00:14:36,881 --> 00:14:39,320
that two fleets were heading toward destinations

194
00:14:39,321 --> 00:14:40,640
other than Normandy.

195
00:14:41,260 --> 00:14:46,660
during the night before D-Day, one to Le Havre and one to Calais.

196
00:14:46,661 --> 00:14:49,700
To create the illusion of a fleet heading for Calais,

197
00:14:49,701 --> 00:14:53,760
a small group of Lancaster bombers of the 617 Squadron

198
00:14:53,761 --> 00:14:56,340
left an airfield in England in the middle of the night

199
00:14:56,341 --> 00:14:57,540
and headed across the channel.

200
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:05,080
And what the 617 Squadron aircraft and their crews did

201
00:15:05,081 --> 00:15:10,100
was to fly in a very specific racetrack course

202
00:15:10,101 --> 00:15:15,580
around and around and around, and each time that they went around in a certain direction

203
00:15:15,581 --> 00:15:22,240
toward the French shore, they advanced it maybe 500 to 600 yards.

204
00:15:22,241 --> 00:15:26,400
When they came to the point where they were heading straight toward the target, they would

205
00:15:26,401 --> 00:15:31,680
drop pieces of aluminum strips, which were called chaff in those days, or window.

206
00:15:31,681 --> 00:15:38,380
In consequence, you have virtually a shower of this chaff or window coming down and advancing

207
00:15:38,381 --> 00:15:42,580
at eight knots, which the Germans knew was the typical speed

208
00:15:42,581 --> 00:15:46,300
that a convoy would make on its way toward an invasion.

209
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:50,860
When the Ghost Fleet got close to France,

210
00:15:50,861 --> 00:15:52,840
the planes pulled away,

211
00:15:52,841 --> 00:15:54,940
and boats below continued the deception.

212
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:01,980
The small launches put out all sorts of smoke screens

213
00:16:01,981 --> 00:16:04,880
so that the Germans couldn't see visibly what was going on.

214
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:07,400
And then they had loudspeakers,

215
00:16:07,401 --> 00:16:09,460
and the loudspeakers made big clacking sounds

216
00:16:09,461 --> 00:16:11,300
as if anchors were being dropped into the water.

217
00:16:12,020 --> 00:16:16,320
They even had splashes, shouts of ensigns and so on giving orders,

218
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:19,400
and it sounded as if a huge fleet was assembling out there

219
00:16:19,401 --> 00:16:20,760
ready to make the invasion.

220
00:16:22,120 --> 00:16:25,300
As the Germans were being duped into believing invasion fleets

221
00:16:25,301 --> 00:16:26,900
were heading to other landing spots,

222
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:29,860
behind the coastal defenses at Normandy,

223
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:32,560
paratroopers were dropping from the night sky.

224
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:35,280
But only some of them were real.

225
00:16:35,281 --> 00:16:46,529
In another deception called Operation Titanic, British-designed dummy paratroopers nicknamed Ruperts were being thrown from planes to draw the Germans' attention away

226
00:16:46,530 --> 00:16:48,020
from the actual drops.

227
00:16:49,100 --> 00:16:50,852
Ruperts are very simple.

228
00:16:50,853 --> 00:16:57,640
They're a burlap sack that vaguely resembles a person about three feet tall with a parachute.

229
00:16:57,641 --> 00:17:08,440
When you consider what a Rupert looks like, the idea that anyone would be fooled for very long was just meaningless.

230
00:17:08,800 --> 00:17:18,360
Although being a typical German, guarding the coast, start seeing parachutes with what appear to be people coming down,

231
00:17:18,820 --> 00:17:25,040
you don't necessarily go over to that field to look and see if the paratroopers have come down.

232
00:17:25,041 --> 00:17:29,480
and you're more apt to go call higher headquarters and tell them you've got a problem on your hands.

233
00:17:32,020 --> 00:17:36,180
Paratroopers weren't the only Allied forces to land behind enemy lines in the middle of the night.

234
00:17:37,020 --> 00:17:42,560
Gliders swooped down silently and deployed men and equipment to help destroy defenses and secure bridges.

235
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:51,880
Glider flying was very similar to paratroops, except we could carry in large equipment.

236
00:17:51,881 --> 00:18:03,560
We were able to get into places, small fields, beyond the enemy, into areas that planes couldn't land, and unload heavy equipment.

237
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:11,740
We're talking about artillery, jeeps, and military equipment that's needed by the paratroops that already dropped in there.

238
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:16,380
Both British and American-built gliders were involved.

239
00:18:16,381 --> 00:18:24,780
the american cg4a also called a waco for the company that designed it

240
00:18:24,781 --> 00:18:31,400
consisted of a canvas covering over a steel and wood frame to keep it as light as possible

241
00:18:31,401 --> 00:18:37,500
it carried no defensive guns or armor but that wasn't why it was nicknamed the flying coffin

242
00:18:37,501 --> 00:18:47,960
When the program started and they needed to have different companies manufacture CG4A gliders,

243
00:18:47,961 --> 00:18:55,200
any company that had experience in plywood and fabric and so forth got a contract to make parts.

244
00:18:55,900 --> 00:19:01,560
And a lot of the companies were coffin makers since they had the experience in making things out of wood.

245
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,620
And so consequently it got the name of Flying Coffin.

246
00:19:04,621 --> 00:19:10,120
The Waco glider has a wingspan of 83 feet 8 inches.

247
00:19:10,121 --> 00:19:14,380
It has a length of 48 feet.

248
00:19:14,381 --> 00:19:18,800
It has an empty weight of 3,750 pounds.

249
00:19:18,801 --> 00:19:28,940
It's capable of carrying that same weight, which means that fully loaded, a Waco is carrying 7,500 pounds of weight for its wing.

250
00:19:28,941 --> 00:19:38,160
with the waco glider the front end the nose if you will will open up so now you basically have

251
00:19:38,161 --> 00:19:44,800
an open box with a ramp you can drive a jeep into or back up the jeep into or push an anti-tank gun

252
00:19:44,801 --> 00:19:52,360
or an artillery piece up into block it and secure it tie it down for flight the american cg4a wacos

253
00:19:52,361 --> 00:19:59,880
were towed into action by C-47 transport planes and attached by 300-foot long nylon tow lines.

254
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:07,000
The gliders used the same controls as a conventional aircraft. Elevators, ailerons,

255
00:20:08,600 --> 00:20:13,720
and a rudder. The instrument panel on the glider consisted of only five gauges.

256
00:20:14,520 --> 00:20:20,360
They kept track of basic flight information, including airspeed, rate of climb, and altitude.

257
00:20:21,020 --> 00:20:26,820
While being towed, glider pilots had to be more concerned about their relationship to their tow plane than their gauges.

258
00:20:28,440 --> 00:20:35,100
Normally, in flight, the C-47 is flying just below where the glider is.

259
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:40,060
The reason being that if you're down behind it, you're in his prop wash, and so you're kind of flopping around.

260
00:20:40,120 --> 00:20:43,220
So if you're flying above, you're out of his prop wash.

261
00:20:43,221 --> 00:20:51,560
The lines of communication between the glider and the tow plane utilized a cord that ran along the tow line

262
00:20:51,561 --> 00:20:57,260
for the simple reason that radio communication is interceptable.

263
00:20:58,080 --> 00:21:01,020
And any time you have radio communication between two planes,

264
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:03,600
that means somebody on the ground can be listening in on you.

265
00:21:04,460 --> 00:21:09,100
The audio cable running alongside the more elastic tow line was often damaged

266
00:21:09,101 --> 00:21:12,100
and communications between planes cut off.

267
00:21:12,760 --> 00:21:14,060
It wasn't a critical failure,

268
00:21:14,660 --> 00:21:17,080
because the most important message from the C-47,

269
00:21:17,660 --> 00:21:19,380
the order to release from the tow line,

270
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:21,720
was delivered in a fail-safe way.

271
00:21:22,740 --> 00:21:26,460
They signaled us with a light out of their dome in the C-47.

272
00:21:27,160 --> 00:21:28,900
They gave us a signal.

273
00:21:29,060 --> 00:21:32,820
We had about 10 seconds, which was a red light,

274
00:21:32,821 --> 00:21:35,540
and then the green light came on, and we cut loose.

275
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:39,680
And you didn't want to hang past the green light,

276
00:21:40,100 --> 00:21:41,680
because if you did, they'd cut you loose.

277
00:21:42,100 --> 00:21:50,240
The Waco glider was quite maneuverable, but carried a maximum passenger load of only 13 fully armed men.

278
00:21:53,260 --> 00:21:59,020
The British Horsa glider could carry double the cargo, but its design had its own drawbacks.

279
00:22:00,400 --> 00:22:07,280
The Horsa having a wingspan only four feet larger than the Waco, but weighing twice as much,

280
00:22:07,740 --> 00:22:09,720
means you've got a higher wing loading.

281
00:22:09,721 --> 00:22:13,800
That means you've got more weight per square inch of that wing.

282
00:22:14,460 --> 00:22:18,440
And in order to maintain flight, that means you've got to fly faster.

283
00:22:18,980 --> 00:22:21,620
And you come in faster and harder.

284
00:22:29,160 --> 00:22:33,460
Many of the gliders, British and American, suffered severe landings.

285
00:22:34,220 --> 00:22:36,340
There was less moonlight than hoped for.

286
00:22:36,341 --> 00:22:40,600
and the fields were smaller and lined with more tall growth than expected.

287
00:22:42,840 --> 00:22:45,320
Even though almost all of the planes were damaged,

288
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:48,740
most of the men and equipment managed to survive.

289
00:22:49,440 --> 00:22:52,080
The troops carried out their missions to capture objectives

290
00:22:52,081 --> 00:22:55,440
and hinder the Germans' ability to respond to the invasion forces

291
00:22:55,441 --> 00:22:58,100
that were about to tear into the beaches

292
00:22:58,101 --> 00:23:01,280
with the explosive technologies of bombs,

293
00:23:02,340 --> 00:23:03,040
artillery,

294
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:05,920
and a device to hand-deliver TNT.

295
00:23:06,340 --> 00:23:15,400
The engine-less CG4A Waco glider, while appearing simple in its design, is actually made up of

296
00:23:15,401 --> 00:23:19,160
over 70,000 parts.

297
00:23:19,161 --> 00:23:25,160
D-Day Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

298
00:23:25,161 --> 00:23:31,980
We now return to D-Day Tech on Modern Marvels.

299
00:23:31,981 --> 00:23:35,020
Dawn, June 6th.

300
00:23:35,021 --> 00:23:38,660
The amphibious assault of Normandy, codenamed Neptune,

301
00:23:38,661 --> 00:23:42,300
had been delayed by one day because of storms.

302
00:23:42,301 --> 00:23:47,960
The seas were still choppy and the weather far from ideal.

303
00:23:47,961 --> 00:23:50,900
Allied bombers roared overhead in a final attempt

304
00:23:50,901 --> 00:23:53,500
to weaken German coastal defenses,

305
00:23:53,501 --> 00:23:57,940
but many missed their targets due to the low cloud cover.

306
00:23:57,941 --> 00:24:01,640
Then it was the fleet's turn to open fire.

307
00:24:01,641 --> 00:24:05,020
Warships and the fearsome new technology of landing craft

308
00:24:05,021 --> 00:24:08,740
armed with over a thousand rockets each, bombarded the coast.

309
00:24:09,700 --> 00:24:14,060
The goal? To knock out enemy guns, blow holes through beach obstacles,

310
00:24:15,020 --> 00:24:18,980
set off mines, and kill or unnerve as many of the enemy as possible.

311
00:24:20,320 --> 00:24:23,540
It was the 1944 version of shock and awe.

312
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:30,980
During the deafening barrage, men clambered off the transport ships

313
00:24:30,981 --> 00:24:33,960
into smaller landing craft that would take them to shore.

314
00:24:33,961 --> 00:24:37,960
When the Allied guns halted, it was time to hit the beaches.

315
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:43,840
The British came ashore at gold and sword.

316
00:24:45,480 --> 00:24:47,200
The Canadians at Juneau.

317
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:50,560
And the Americans at Utah and Omaha.

318
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,440
The men at Omaha had it the roughest.

319
00:24:54,540 --> 00:24:59,480
Many of the German obstacles and gun positions had survived the opening air and naval barrages.

320
00:24:59,481 --> 00:25:08,720
One of the units first ashore are the amphibious engineers, and their job is to go ashore and blow gaps through the enemy wire.

321
00:25:09,260 --> 00:25:13,900
You know, here the Germans have lain strands of bob wire entanglements up and down the beach.

322
00:25:14,020 --> 00:25:18,120
Well, your men can't just run through that, so they have to take out the wire.

323
00:25:18,300 --> 00:25:20,240
How do they do that? Bangalore torpedoes.

324
00:25:21,300 --> 00:25:28,980
Bangalore torpedoes, five-foot lengths of two-and-an-eighth-inch diameter pipe packed with explosive, were simple yet effective.

325
00:25:29,480 --> 00:25:36,480
They're light enough that a man could carry one or two pieces, and so a squad or a platoon

326
00:25:36,481 --> 00:25:38,940
could carry a number of pieces.

327
00:25:38,941 --> 00:25:45,500
And when the path was needed through a barbed wire entanglement, you put on a nose cap and

328
00:25:45,501 --> 00:25:52,560
you kept pushing ahead pipe after pipe after pipe, put a blasting cap in your last piece

329
00:25:52,561 --> 00:25:55,360
of the Bangalore torpedo, set it off.

330
00:25:55,361 --> 00:26:04,300
The steel from the pipe as well as the explosion would blow a gap right through the barbed wire and also explode any mines in the gap.

331
00:26:04,920 --> 00:26:06,680
And that gave you a way to move forward.

332
00:26:10,100 --> 00:26:19,460
Once beyond the barbed wire, the Allies used 10-foot wooden rods with TNT attached to one end, called pole charges, to take out enemy bunkers.

333
00:26:19,461 --> 00:26:23,980
and the idea here is if you want to blow a hole through an enemy bunker,

334
00:26:24,780 --> 00:26:30,040
the assaulting infantryman or engineer has to run up under fire to the bunker,

335
00:26:30,760 --> 00:26:36,440
place the pole up against the bunker, knock one end firmly into the ground,

336
00:26:36,580 --> 00:26:41,920
and as he does that, the hinge plate will swing up flush with the bunker,

337
00:26:41,921 --> 00:26:45,620
and here you have all these blocks of TNT now firmly against the bunker.

338
00:26:46,100 --> 00:26:48,700
At that time, if he hasn't been killed by the Germans,

339
00:26:49,100 --> 00:26:51,940
we'll set the fuse and run like hell,

340
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:54,620
at which time the pull charge will go off

341
00:26:54,621 --> 00:26:56,900
and hopefully blow a hole through the enemy bunker.

342
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:01,420
Bringing the men to shore at Omaha

343
00:27:01,421 --> 00:27:04,420
was the smallest of the landing craft used that day,

344
00:27:04,920 --> 00:27:05,960
the LCVP.

345
00:27:07,420 --> 00:27:09,740
Each one carried just 36 men,

346
00:27:10,560 --> 00:27:12,920
but the flat-bottom boat rode high in the water,

347
00:27:12,921 --> 00:27:15,920
got the men past many of the submerged obstacles

348
00:27:15,921 --> 00:27:19,040
and delivered them right up to the water's edge.

349
00:27:20,680 --> 00:27:24,100
The 36 men inside the squad bay

350
00:27:24,101 --> 00:27:27,000
could stand upright with packs on their backs,

351
00:27:27,100 --> 00:27:27,960
rifles in their hands,

352
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:30,420
and when the boat beached itself and dropped the ramp,

353
00:27:30,840 --> 00:27:32,540
they could unload from the boat

354
00:27:32,541 --> 00:27:34,060
in a very, very short space of time.

355
00:27:34,140 --> 00:27:35,620
They could be off and in the battle.

356
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:40,520
The LCVP, also known as the Higgins Boat,

357
00:27:40,521 --> 00:27:43,020
in honor of Louisiana shipbuilder Andrew Higgins,

358
00:27:43,800 --> 00:27:47,780
was an adaptation of a pre-war swamp boat he designed for use in Bayou country.

359
00:27:51,280 --> 00:27:56,160
Higgins designed boats that were particularly adept at operating in shallow tributaries,

360
00:27:56,340 --> 00:27:57,620
in other words, swampy areas.

361
00:27:57,621 --> 00:28:03,340
And because of this early experience in designing boats that could operate effectively in shallow water,

362
00:28:03,720 --> 00:28:06,480
it was really a very simple transition for Higgins then

363
00:28:06,481 --> 00:28:10,520
to begin building boats that were capable of landing assault forces.

364
00:28:13,100 --> 00:28:17,880
It's got a big six-cylinder gray marine engine in it with a twin-disc transmission.

365
00:28:18,660 --> 00:28:24,560
The transmission is really unique in that it can go from forward to reverse at full power.

366
00:28:25,160 --> 00:28:32,280
This allowed easy reverse from the beach so the boat could get off the beach once it dropped its load

367
00:28:32,281 --> 00:28:34,320
and go back and bring in more troops.

368
00:28:34,321 --> 00:28:43,800
The prop design of the LCVP was a channel prop that ran down through the hull and had a protective keel spine that ran out to the back of the boat.

369
00:28:44,040 --> 00:28:46,520
So the prop was somewhat enclosed and protected.

370
00:28:47,200 --> 00:28:53,800
It has an iron ramp in the front that was lowered by a series of winches and pulleys that were in the inside of the landing craft.

371
00:28:54,680 --> 00:28:57,980
But the hull of the landing craft was made of marine mahogany.

372
00:28:58,140 --> 00:29:01,500
It's basically a wooden boat with a steel ramp.

373
00:29:01,501 --> 00:29:08,500
Higgins Industries in New Orleans worked around the clock with a workforce that grew to 30,000

374
00:29:08,501 --> 00:29:13,500
men and women to supply nearly 20,000 boats for the war effort.

375
00:29:13,501 --> 00:29:19,500
The importance of the boats that Higgins built was best summed up in the words of General Eisenhower.

376
00:29:19,501 --> 00:29:26,500
If Higgins had not designed and built those LCVPs, we never could have landed over an open beach.

377
00:29:26,501 --> 00:29:30,500
The whole strategy of the war would have been different.

378
00:29:31,500 --> 00:29:43,638
With the first wave of men gaining a foothold on the beaches of Normandy, the next wave of machines, from huge cavernous landing ships to strange hybrid vehicles, was

379
00:29:43,639 --> 00:29:45,320
about to rumble ashore.

380
00:29:48,660 --> 00:29:59,860
The afternoon of June 6, 1944, the German beach defenses at Normandy had been overwhelmed by a potent mix of advanced wartime technologies and bravery.

381
00:29:59,861 --> 00:30:05,900
an astounding number of landing craft and supply ships converged on the coast of Normandy.

382
00:30:06,820 --> 00:30:07,520
Their job?

383
00:30:07,521 --> 00:30:13,660
To start unloading the equipment and men needed to establish a beachhead and prepare for the push inland.

384
00:30:15,180 --> 00:30:18,980
When I looked back from the beach to the channels, the sea,

385
00:30:19,700 --> 00:30:25,980
I was shocked by the number of crafts and ships. I couldn't see the horizon.

386
00:30:25,981 --> 00:30:32,260
There were battleships and cruisers and all kinds of landing craft coming in and going out.

387
00:30:33,420 --> 00:30:38,060
And I remember thinking at that time that this is the high point of my life.

388
00:30:38,220 --> 00:30:40,220
This is a tremendous operation.

389
00:30:42,100 --> 00:30:45,700
Over 5,000 vessels took part in the landings at Normandy.

390
00:30:46,400 --> 00:30:52,860
At 328 feet, the American-built LST, or landing ship tank, was the biggest.

391
00:30:52,861 --> 00:30:55,940
It had a drive-on, drive-off capability.

392
00:30:56,540 --> 00:31:00,920
What this did was it made it possible for the LST to take on a load of vehicles

393
00:31:00,921 --> 00:31:03,320
that could be everything from two-and-a-half-ton trucks,

394
00:31:03,720 --> 00:31:05,840
or they could even be a platoon of Sherman tanks.

395
00:31:06,680 --> 00:31:08,720
The LST had two decks.

396
00:31:09,480 --> 00:31:11,700
The lighter vehicles were loaded onto the top deck,

397
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:14,540
while the tanks and other heavy equipment stayed below.

398
00:31:15,640 --> 00:31:19,180
The craft's flat bottom enabled it to slide right up to a beach.

399
00:31:19,181 --> 00:31:26,020
The ballast tanks could also be adjusted so that your bow could be raised somewhat,

400
00:31:26,660 --> 00:31:31,600
and the stern lowered a little bit, so that when it came into Normandy shore, for example,

401
00:31:32,160 --> 00:31:36,460
it would be sort of like a goose landing slowly, tilted somewhat backwards,

402
00:31:36,600 --> 00:31:39,020
and you could come in closer for the offloading of your equipment.

403
00:31:40,940 --> 00:31:44,920
Once beached, the LST's large clamshell doors were opened,

404
00:31:45,440 --> 00:31:48,360
and the ramp lowered for the quick unloading of its enormous cargo.

405
00:31:49,180 --> 00:31:56,880
If the angle of the beach kept the huge LSTs from getting close enough to shore, Rhino ferries

406
00:31:56,881 --> 00:32:00,000
were used to haul the equipment to land.

407
00:32:00,001 --> 00:32:06,820
Built specifically for D-Day, they were 41 feet wide and 176 feet long and made from

408
00:32:06,821 --> 00:32:07,820
steel pontoons.

409
00:32:07,821 --> 00:32:11,900
They were powered by two large outboard engines.

410
00:32:11,901 --> 00:32:17,080
LSTs were equipped with a dock that Rhinos were able to latch onto.

411
00:32:17,081 --> 00:32:25,040
You could actually drive this pier up to an LST, open the doors of the LST, lock on with

412
00:32:25,041 --> 00:32:29,700
the Rhino barge, the tank could drive right onto this barge, and the Rhino barge could

413
00:32:29,701 --> 00:32:33,620
take the tank dry right to the beach.

414
00:32:33,621 --> 00:32:41,720
When they crossed the channel with the invasion fleet, each Rhino ferry carried its own bulldozer.

415
00:32:41,721 --> 00:32:44,160
half-loaded on the first trip to the beach.

416
00:32:44,161 --> 00:32:48,660
The dozers would build sand ramps to aid in unloading

417
00:32:48,661 --> 00:32:53,820
and help push the barge back to sea if it became grounded.

418
00:32:53,821 --> 00:32:56,840
Another of the indispensable transports used at Normandy

419
00:32:56,841 --> 00:33:02,680
was a half-truck, half-boat that could do the job of both.

420
00:33:02,681 --> 00:33:06,820
In 1942, General Motors, which was building the Deuce and a Half,

421
00:33:06,821 --> 00:33:09,400
the two and a half ton truck that was the backbone of our Army

422
00:33:09,401 --> 00:33:12,560
Transport, decided to come up with an amphibious version.

423
00:33:15,240 --> 00:33:19,540
It was given a corporate designation of D-U-K-W,

424
00:33:19,940 --> 00:33:23,900
but anything named D-U-K-W was quickly dubbed by the Army Duck.

425
00:33:26,580 --> 00:33:29,560
The 31-foot-long vehicle was shaped like a boat

426
00:33:29,561 --> 00:33:32,500
and had a hollow, airtight body for buoyancy.

427
00:33:33,240 --> 00:33:35,940
A single propeller provided the duck's momentum in the water.

428
00:33:35,941 --> 00:33:40,260
while it used six-wheel drive on land.

429
00:33:43,080 --> 00:33:49,020
It had the same six-cylinder engine that was used in the deuce-and-a-half truck.

430
00:33:49,160 --> 00:33:52,240
It was a heavy-duty truck engine, very powerful.

431
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:56,240
On road, it could give you the same performance as a truck.

432
00:33:56,420 --> 00:33:58,020
It could go 55 miles an hour.

433
00:33:58,420 --> 00:34:03,140
It wasn't wise to go that fast, but it would cruise all day at 35 without any trouble.

434
00:34:03,141 --> 00:34:10,140
On the water, depending on the water conditions, it would go five, six, maybe eight knots.

435
00:34:11,240 --> 00:34:13,460
It was not a speedster. It was not meant to be.

436
00:34:16,220 --> 00:34:23,060
With their ability to transport weapons, troops, and supplies from offshore ships directly to supply depots or fighting units,

437
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:27,680
Ducks played a vital role in the complex logistics at Normandy.

438
00:34:27,681 --> 00:34:33,440
When enough armored vehicles and men were unloaded on the beaches of France,

439
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:36,900
the invasion force began to penetrate the French countryside.

440
00:34:38,440 --> 00:34:41,160
Here, the troops encountered natural obstructions

441
00:34:41,161 --> 00:34:45,320
that proved to be as problematic as any of Rommel's contraptions on the beaches.

442
00:34:46,300 --> 00:34:47,000
Hedgerows.

443
00:34:48,300 --> 00:34:55,120
A hedgerow, it's an extended shrubbery that's been growing for about 600 or 700 years.

444
00:34:55,121 --> 00:35:02,300
and it's usually, although not invariably, but a lot of times the basis for it was a crumbling stone wall.

445
00:35:04,080 --> 00:35:09,980
The problem with the hedgerows was that if you used a, if you tried to scale them by tanks,

446
00:35:10,480 --> 00:35:13,500
the tanks moving up and over them, some of them 8, 10 feet tall,

447
00:35:13,800 --> 00:35:19,320
it exposed the tank's soft underbellying and made them easy targets for German anti-tank weapons.

448
00:35:19,321 --> 00:35:29,440
If allied tanks couldn't go over or around the hedgerows without being exposed to enemy fire,

449
00:35:30,380 --> 00:35:33,520
the only choice was to find a way to plow through them.

450
00:35:34,040 --> 00:35:39,900
The solution was created by the men in the field with a makeshift modification made from scavenged metal parts.

451
00:35:40,900 --> 00:35:46,020
Well, you just take a steel plate and you weld the prongs to the bottom of it and it's fixed on the tank.

452
00:35:46,021 --> 00:35:49,020
and you get it down low enough so the tank can get underneath,

453
00:35:49,220 --> 00:35:51,860
basically scoop and just pull the stuff out by the roots,

454
00:35:52,000 --> 00:35:53,740
just like you would do if you were clearing brush.

455
00:35:55,540 --> 00:35:59,280
To me, a lot of this is what we call spontaneous innovation.

456
00:36:03,300 --> 00:36:05,640
I mean, the biggest innovative force that we've got

457
00:36:05,641 --> 00:36:08,220
is basically just ordinary American soldiers

458
00:36:08,221 --> 00:36:12,160
who come in from civilian life and they're trying to solve these problems.

459
00:36:12,161 --> 00:36:19,160
This sort of on-the-spot ingenuity gave the Allied forces the edge on a day-to-day basis.

460
00:36:19,161 --> 00:36:26,160
But two mammoth long-term engineering undertakings, built in secrecy,

461
00:36:26,161 --> 00:36:35,160
would help guarantee that the D-Day landings were just the beginning of the liberation of Europe.

462
00:36:35,161 --> 00:36:45,160
The dashboard of a DUKW or DUCK featured the controls of a tire inflation system.

463
00:36:45,161 --> 00:36:53,160
This enabled the driver to inflate or deflate any or all of the vehicle's six tires to adjust for different terrain.

464
00:36:53,161 --> 00:36:58,160
D-Day Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

465
00:36:58,161 --> 00:37:02,340
As allied men and equipment inundated the beaches of Normandy,

466
00:37:02,341 --> 00:37:08,940
the Germans still thought that the D-Day landings might be just a diversion for the real invasion

467
00:37:08,941 --> 00:37:10,473
force yet to come.

468
00:37:10,474 --> 00:37:16,860
It just didn't make sense to land where there wasn't a harbor to facilitate

469
00:37:16,861 --> 00:37:22,300
the unloading of all the men and materiel necessary to gain a foothold in France.

470
00:37:22,301 --> 00:37:29,020
Little did the Germans know that a pair of the biggest secrets of the war, the two portable

471
00:37:29,021 --> 00:37:35,920
harbors named Mulberries, were being towed into position.

472
00:37:35,921 --> 00:37:41,640
Mulberry A would serve the American landings at Omaha and Utah beaches, and Mulberry B

473
00:37:41,641 --> 00:37:44,840
would be assembled off Gold Beach by British forces.

474
00:37:44,841 --> 00:37:51,840
In the rough seas of the Channel, an effective breakwater was the most vital part of the

475
00:37:51,841 --> 00:37:54,409
puzzle that made up the Mulberries.

476
00:37:54,410 --> 00:37:58,080
It was for this purpose that a series of up to 200

477
00:37:58,081 --> 00:38:04,080
foot long and 50 foot wide concrete caissons, code named Phoenix's, were built.

478
00:38:04,081 --> 00:38:11,760
They brought them across the English Channel by large tugboats, positioned them where they

479
00:38:11,761 --> 00:38:20,161
want to have the breakwater, and smaller tugboats would position them very accurately.

480
00:38:20,162 --> 00:38:21,040
Then they

481
00:38:21,041 --> 00:38:24,540
They would open up the C valves underneath them and sink them.

482
00:38:24,541 --> 00:38:30,680
Of course, they had to know the depth of the channel there, which I understood that the

483
00:38:30,681 --> 00:38:34,280
Royal Engineers had all that surveyed.

484
00:38:34,281 --> 00:38:43,840
To create secondary breakwaters on the outer edges of the harbors, old ships were scuttled.

485
00:38:43,841 --> 00:38:52,120
Along the areas that they wanted to protect, they sank merchant ships, old battleships,

486
00:38:52,121 --> 00:38:55,080
anything they could find that they didn't need anymore.

487
00:38:55,081 --> 00:38:59,120
Some of the ships were sunk in relatively shallow water so that the decks were still

488
00:38:59,121 --> 00:39:04,060
above water, and they were manned with anti-aircraft guns and crews for a period of time after

489
00:39:04,061 --> 00:39:06,840
the invasion.

490
00:39:06,841 --> 00:39:11,520
To provide a dock for ships to unload on, steel rectangular floating pier heads were

491
00:39:11,521 --> 00:39:14,160
were built, with legs that allowed their platforms

492
00:39:14,161 --> 00:39:15,580
to rise and lower with the tides.

493
00:39:18,360 --> 00:39:23,340
Each mulberry utilized six miles of flexible steel roadway.

494
00:39:23,341 --> 00:39:27,540
Flotation was provided by steel and concrete pontoons.

495
00:39:27,541 --> 00:39:29,880
This enabled the men and equipment being unloaded

496
00:39:29,881 --> 00:39:32,440
at the pier heads to drive over the water

497
00:39:32,441 --> 00:39:34,620
and arrive on dry land.

498
00:39:34,621 --> 00:39:37,920
On June 12, after only a week, the American and British

499
00:39:37,921 --> 00:39:40,600
mulberries were operational.

500
00:39:40,601 --> 00:39:43,500
And we could unload a huge amount of material,

501
00:39:43,501 --> 00:39:46,200
just as if it was a natural part or harbor.

502
00:39:46,201 --> 00:39:47,820
It was one of the most creative things

503
00:39:47,821 --> 00:39:51,400
that we did during the D-Day invasion.

504
00:39:51,401 --> 00:39:54,100
But after only a week, disaster struck.

505
00:39:58,780 --> 00:40:01,960
The worst storm in 40 years came up

506
00:40:01,961 --> 00:40:06,760
and began to immediately cause casualties and damage

507
00:40:06,761 --> 00:40:09,800
to resources, most importantly to Mulberry Day.

508
00:40:10,600 --> 00:40:18,500
The storm continued for several days, and the outer floating seawall started to break

509
00:40:18,501 --> 00:40:19,500
apart.

510
00:40:19,501 --> 00:40:25,860
Big pieces of this began hitting against Mulberry A and damaging and ultimately destroying

511
00:40:25,861 --> 00:40:26,860
most of it.

512
00:40:26,861 --> 00:40:33,480
The American Mulberry was beyond repair, and the British Mulberry was badly damaged.

513
00:40:33,481 --> 00:40:38,200
The Allies have lost half of their port facilities.

514
00:40:38,201 --> 00:40:42,780
So what they do is they take what's left of Mulberry A, the American Mulberry Harbor,

515
00:40:43,100 --> 00:40:46,400
they bring it over to the British beaches, Mulberry B,

516
00:40:47,040 --> 00:40:50,700
to help build that harbor back up and get that functioning again.

517
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:55,880
There was one more major technological challenge for the Allies to meet.

518
00:40:56,760 --> 00:41:01,220
How to guarantee a steady supply of fuel to the mechanized armies landing in France.

519
00:41:01,221 --> 00:41:07,000
The innovative solution was called Pluto, short for Pipeline Under the Ocean.

520
00:41:08,200 --> 00:41:13,340
In building an undersea pipeline, which is what they had to do, they had to come up with new technology.

521
00:41:14,120 --> 00:41:18,300
And rather than create something wholly new, they went to what they knew worked,

522
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:20,780
which was undersea cables, transatlantic cables,

523
00:41:21,140 --> 00:41:27,420
which were flexible cables that were in short segments connected and protected by a rubberized coating.

524
00:41:29,120 --> 00:41:36,180
For Pluto, three-inch diameter steel and lead pipes were welded in lengths as long as 30 miles.

525
00:41:38,200 --> 00:41:47,240
They were wrapped in a protective and flexible coating that included layers of paper, steel

526
00:41:47,241 --> 00:41:52,380
tape and steel armor wire.

527
00:41:52,381 --> 00:41:57,760
The entire project was ready by D-Day, but deployment was delayed for nearly a month

528
00:41:57,761 --> 00:42:02,820
because the Allies had difficulty capturing the planned destination site, the port city

529
00:42:02,821 --> 00:42:03,820
of Cherbourg.

530
00:42:08,200 --> 00:42:12,200
The heavy German resistance finally gave way on June 27th,

531
00:42:12,201 --> 00:42:16,200
but the Germans destroyed much of the port before relinquishing control.

532
00:42:18,200 --> 00:42:21,200
When Cherbourg was sufficiently rebuilt to receive Pluto,

533
00:42:21,201 --> 00:42:26,200
it only took about 10 hours to lay the pipelines across the 70 miles of channel.

534
00:42:27,200 --> 00:42:30,200
The lead pipes were deployed from the holds of ships,

535
00:42:32,200 --> 00:42:37,200
while the steel pipelines were unrolled off huge floating drums called conundrums.

536
00:42:38,200 --> 00:42:45,200
The first Pluto pipeline became operational on August 12, 1944.

537
00:42:45,201 --> 00:42:49,200
During the next several months, over 500 miles of pipeline

538
00:42:49,201 --> 00:42:52,200
were laid across the channel and over land

539
00:42:52,201 --> 00:42:56,200
to keep pace with the forces as they made their way east.

540
00:42:58,200 --> 00:43:01,200
Even though one of the mulberries had been destroyed by a storm

541
00:43:01,201 --> 00:43:05,200
and the initial laying of the underwater pipeline had been delayed,

542
00:43:05,201 --> 00:43:09,460
These two engineering marvels helped the Allies ride toward victory.

543
00:43:13,540 --> 00:43:18,660
They joined all the new machines, electronic trickery, and scientific breakthroughs

544
00:43:18,661 --> 00:43:23,340
as part of the Herculean effort that went into the success of D-Day.

545
00:43:25,260 --> 00:43:28,580
Technology was an important tool to achieving victory,

546
00:43:29,040 --> 00:43:32,100
and it was necessary because without the technology,

547
00:43:32,101 --> 00:43:36,980
the cemeteries that overlook Omaha Beach would have been much larger than they are.

548
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:46,860
It still stands alone among the world's great military enterprises

549
00:43:46,861 --> 00:43:54,600
as a vast, complex, and nevertheless a human endeavor.

550
00:43:54,601 --> 00:44:04,600
With the D-Day landings, the Allied invading force had successfully breached Fortress Europe,

551
00:44:04,601 --> 00:44:09,600
would soon liberate France, and then roll toward Berlin.

552
00:44:09,601 --> 00:44:14,600
The technology the Allies developed for the ultimate military assault helped give them

553
00:44:14,601 --> 00:44:17,600
the advantage and change the direction of the war.

